            <div xml:id="d509">

                <pb facs="0596" n="501" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg501"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6241: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-10-17T12:00:00-05:00">October 17,
                        1936—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>247. Press of October 15 under Shanghai date line October 14 carried United Press
                    and Havas reports to the effect that Kung has confirmed that a loan to China
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) is now under consideration (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) will soon be granted by British Government.</p>
                <p>London Embassy, commenting upon reports in regard to this subject appearing in
                    the American press under London date line October 13, states “informal enquiries
                    at the Export Credit Guarantee Department revealed that whereas Leith-Ross had
                    urged the granting of a materials credit to China, the matter is still under
                    discussion within that Department which maintains that no contact has yet been
                    made with the Chinese authorities since the proposal has not yet taken precise
                    form.”</p>
                <p>Telegraph available information in regard to foregoing and keep Department
                    currently informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
